    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Brice Turang** (73.40) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (70.39) headline our lineup as the highest-efficiency plays. **Brice Turang** benefits immensely from a soft matchup against Rhett Lowder (5.10 xERA), with his elevated position in the two-hole providing the optimal environment to capitalize on his elite sprint speed and category scarcity. Meanwhile, **Tyler Soderstrom** enters a favorable situation against Tyler Mahle; the combination of a projected cleanup role and a park factor bolstered by favorable wind conditions pushes him past the 70-point threshold, making him a cornerstone of today\u2019s offensive strategy. **Corbin Carroll** (66.84) and **James Wood** (62.42) round out our top-tier contributors, with the latter remaining a high-ceiling play despite a daunting matchup against Aaron Nola, anchored by his elite 0.404 xwOBA.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Zach McKinstry**, who narrowly missed the cut with a 46.90 score. While his matchup against Ryan Weathers offers a favorable park factor and a situational boost due to a slower opposing pitcher\u2014which historically aids his stolen base upside\u2014the optimizer ultimately preferred the higher-floor stability of **Kyle Karros** and **Travis Bazzana**. Speaking of **Travis Bazzana**, his start carries the primary risk of the day, as he currently faces a 32% rain threat in his matchup against C. Murphy. Additionally, managers should monitor the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Josh Naylor**, **Max Muncy**, **Austin Riley**, **Jose Caballero**, **Henry Bolte**, and **Tyler Soderstrom**. Should any of these players slide down their respective batting orders, their efficiency scores will adjust downward accordingly. While **Seiya Suzuki** was considered, his sub-45 efficiency score against the tough McLean made him an easy exclusion in favor of higher-projected starters.",
